{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets"]]},"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Guides","title":"Guides","identifiers":["doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/FindingRecords","doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/WhatTheMacrosRefuse","doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/ReplacingARegistry","doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/PlatformsAndLinking"],"generated":true}],"metadata":{"title":"Declaring a kind","roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","modules":[{"name":"LinkerSets"}]},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/DeclaringAKind","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"abstract":[{"text":"Pick the number that tags your declarations, and decide whether your users write it.","type":"text"}],"sections":[],"kind":"article","variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/linkersets\/declaringakind"]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A kind is a 32-bit number that every record carries. A "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/LinkerSet","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":" of a kind holds the records"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"declared with that kind and no others, so one program can hold your routes, another library’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"analytics events and a third’s migrations without any of them seeing the others."}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Choosing the number","anchor":"Choosing-the-number","level":3},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Any number other than 0 works. 0 marks an empty slot, and the macros refuse it. The convention is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"four ASCII letters that name the thing, read as one byte each: “rout” is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"r"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"o"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"u"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"t"},{"type":"text","text":", which is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0x72"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0x6F"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0x75"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0x74"},{"type":"text","text":", so the kind is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0x726F_7574"},{"type":"text","text":". The letters make it unlikely that two"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"libraries pick the same number, and they stay readable in a hex dump of the binary. Swift computes"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one for you:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let kind = \"rout\".utf8.reduce(0) { $0 << 8 | UInt32($1) }  \/\/ 0x726F_7574"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Give the kind a name for the code that reads it:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["extension LinkerSetKind {","    static let routes = LinkerSetKind(rawValue: 0x726F_7574)  \/\/ \"rout\"","}"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A library that reads the set for its users keeps the name internal: the macro writes the kind as a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"number, so its users’ files never need it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","text":"When two libraries pick the same number","anchor":"When-two-libraries-pick-the-same-number","level":3},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"No compiler sees every module at once, so nothing can check that a kind is unique. If two libraries"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"choose the same number, their records land in the same set. A record still hands over its value"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"only when asked for its own type, so a set of a type your library owns ("},{"code":"Route","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", not "},{"code":"String","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":")"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"holds only your declarations. Read your records as a type of your own, and a clash costs nothing"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"but the time to skip the other library’s records."}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Writing the kind at each declaration","anchor":"Writing-the-kind-at-each-declaration","level":3},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/LinkerSetEntry(kind:)","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" takes the kind as an integer literal. It has to be a literal because the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"macro writes it into the binary at compile time; a named constant isn’t visible to the macro:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["enum Routes {","    @LinkerSetEntry(kind: 0x726F_7574)  \/\/ \"rout\"","    static let home = Route(path: \"\/\")","}"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"That suits a kind used in a few places. A typo in the number puts the declaration in the wrong","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"set without any error, though.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Giving your users a macro","anchor":"Giving-your-users-a-macro","level":3},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A library that asks its users to register things gives them a macro with the kind built in, so","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"they write ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Route","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and never see the number. The macro lives in a small compiler plugin built on","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the LinkerSetsMacroSupport module, whose documentation walks through the plugin, the macro’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"declaration and the package’s targets. The plugin can also require a protocol of every type it","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"registers, so ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Event","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" on a type that isn’t an event fails to compile where it’s written.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"references":{"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/FindingRecords":{"kind":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/FindingRecords","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/findingrecords","role":"article","title":"Finding records","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Read every declaration of a kind, as the type you expect."}],"type":"topic"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/ReplacingARegistry":{"type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Move a central list of event types, routes or registrations onto declarations that find"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"themselves."}],"title":"Replacing a hand-written registry","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/ReplacingARegistry","kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/replacingaregistry","role":"article"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/PlatformsAndLinking":{"kind":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/PlatformsAndLinking","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/platformsandlinking","role":"article","title":"Platforms and linking","abstract":[{"text":"Where records are found, how, and which test shows it.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets":{"title":"LinkerSets","role":"collection","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"Declarations that register themselves at compile time and are found at run time across every module a program links.","type":"text"}]},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/LinkerSetEntry(kind:)":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/LinkerSetEntry(kind:)","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Declares a record of "},{"code":"kind","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" whose value is this declaration’s."}],"url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/linkersetentry(kind:)","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","title":"LinkerSetEntry(kind:)","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"macro","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"LinkerSetEntry","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"(","kind":"text"},{"text":"kind","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"UInt32","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:s6UInt32V"},{"text":")","kind":"text"}]},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/WhatTheMacrosRefuse":{"type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"Every declaration a record can’t describe is a compile error, with a message that says why.","type":"text"}],"title":"What the macros refuse","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/WhatTheMacrosRefuse","kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/whatthemacrosrefuse","role":"article"},"doc://LinkerSets/documentation/LinkerSets/LinkerSet":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"LinkerSet","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/linkersets\/linkerset","identifier":"doc:\/\/LinkerSets\/documentation\/LinkerSets\/LinkerSet","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every record of one kind, across every image the program has loaded, and the values they"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"produce."}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","title":"LinkerSet","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"LinkerSet","kind":"identifier"}]}}}